leihen:
==German==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|de|:inh|gmh:līhen|tree=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|līhen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|līhan}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*līhwan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*līhwaną}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈlaɪ̯ən/}}
* {{audio|de|De-leihen2.ogg|a=<<Germany>> (<<Berlin>>)}}
* {{audio|de|De-leihen.ogg}}
* {{homophones|de|Laien}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|leihen<lieh,geliehen>}}

# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[borrow]]
#: {{syn|de|ausleihen|borgen}}
#* {{quote-journal|de|year=2010|journal=w:Der Spiegel|url=http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/index-2010-25.html|issue=25/2010|page=80
|passage=Ein Verbot sollte es nach Ansicht vieler Ökonomen auch für die sogenannten Leerverkäufe geben. Banken verkaufen dabei Aktien oder Währungen, die sie noch gar nicht besitzen oder allenfalls '''geliehen haben'''.|t=In the opinion of many economists there should also exist a prohibition for the so-called short sales. In these banks sell shares or currencies that they do not own at all yet or have borrowed at best.}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[lend]], [[loan]]
#: {{syn|de|ausleihen|borgen|verleihen}}

====Usage notes====
* The German synonyms {{m|de||leihen}}, {{m|de||ausleihen}}, {{m|de||borgen}} are all used for “to borrow” as well as “to lend”. When both the giver and taker are named, there is no room for ambiguity:
:: {{uxi|de|Ich habe ihm das Buch '''geliehen'''.|I've '''lent''' him the book.}}
:: {{uxi|de|Ich habe (mir) das Buch [[von]]/[[bei]] ihm '''geliehen'''.|I've '''borrowed''' the book from him.}}
* When the above is not the case, the three mentioned verbs are generally all interpreted as “to borrow”, while {{m|de|verleihen}} is used for “to lend”:
:: {{uxi|de|Ich habe das Buch '''geliehen'''.|I've '''borrowed''' the book.}}
:: {{uxi|de|Ich habe das Buch '''verliehen'''.|I've '''lent''' the book (out).}}
* {{m|de||leihen}} and its derivatives are preferred in official contexts, e.g. when checking out books from a library or taking on a loan from a bank.

