|passage={{...}}the character of a horseman was inseparable connected with the ''knight''—the military attendant of the baron, who was himself nothing more than the '''''Wer''''', or ''Man'', of the king—even the ''armiger'', {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1864|author=Thomas Oswald Cockayne|title=Leechdoms, Wortcunning, and Starcraft of Early England [...] from Brit. mus. ms. Cotton. Vitellius C. III|page=205
|passage=Further is this wort of two kinds, '''wer''' and wife, or male and female. The '''wer''', or male, hath white blossoms, and the wife, or female, hath red or brown; either is beneficial and wonderlike, and they have on them wondrous virtue.}}
