From {{inh|de|gmh|wër}}, from {{inh|de|goh|wër}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*hwaʀ}}. Related to {{m|de|wo}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/veːr/|[veːɐ̯]|[vɛɐ̯]|[ʋ-]}}
* {{audio|de|De-wer.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-wer2.ogg|a=<<Germany>> (<<Berlin>>)}}
* {{homophones|de|Wehr}}
* {{rhymes|de|eːɐ̯|s=1}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|de|pronoun|cat2=interrogative pronouns|cat3=relative pronouns}}

# {{lb|de|interrogative}} [[who]] {{gloss|what person or people}}
#: {{uxi|de|'''Wer''' hat das gesagt?|'''Who''' said that?}}
# {{lb|de|interrogative|colloquial}} [[what]], [[which]] (one) ''(see usage notes)''
# {{lb|de|relative}} [[whoever]], [[he]] who, [[someone]] who, the [[person]] who, [[anyone]] who {{gloss|whatever person or persons}}
#: {{ux|de|'''Wer''' eine Frage hat, kann sich jetzt melden.|'''Whoever''' has a question, put up their hands now.}}
#: {{ux|de|[[wer im Glashaus sitzt, soll nicht mit Steinen werfen|'''Wer''' im Glashaus sitzt, soll nicht mit Steinen werfen.]]|'''People who''' live in glass houses shouldn't throw stones.|q=proverb}}
# {{lb|de|indefinite|colloquial}} [[somebody]], [[someone]]; [[anybody]], [[anyone]] {{gloss|an unspecified person}}
#: {{syn|de|jemand|einer}}
#: {{uxi|de|Da ist '''wer''' an der Tür.|There's '''someone''' at the door.}}
#* {{quote-book|de|year=1764|title=Mägera, die förchterliche Hexe|author=Philipp Hafner|location=Vienna|page=53|url=https://www.google.de/books/edition/Ein_neues_Zauberlustspiel_betitelt_M%C3%A4ge/3dJUAAAAcAAJ?gbpv=1&pg=PA53|text={{smallcaps|Riepel.}} Das ist ein Geschrey, als ob '''wer''' gestorben wäre.|t={{smallcaps|Riepel.}} All this wailing, it's as though '''someone''' had died.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|what}} In colloquial German, ''wer'' and its forms may be used in reference to things. This is often done when asking about a noun whose gender one knows to be masculine or feminine. For example: ''Kannst du mir mal den Dings rübergeben?''&nbsp;– '''''Wen'''?'' (“Could you pass me the thingamabob?”&nbsp;– “Pass '''what'''?”) In this case, the interrogative mirrors the grammatical gender of the noun one asks about.
* {{sense|someone}} The colloquial ''wer'' meaning “someone” cannot usually be the first word in a main clause, because it could be misinterpreted as an interrogative in this position. The only exception is when it is followed by a nominalized adjective:
*: '''''Wer''' Neues ist in die Mannschaft gekommen.'' – '''Someone''' new joined the team.

====Declension====
