===Noun===
{{nl-noun|f|-en|+}}

# political power, [[control]]
#: {{ant|nl|onmacht|machteloosheid}}
# [[power]], [[might]], [[capability]]
#: {{syn|nl|vermogen}}
# a [[power]] in international politics
#: {{syn|nl|mogendheid}}
# a military [[force]], such as an army
# {{lb|nl|mathematics}} power (of multiplication)
# {{lb|nl|Christianity}} [[power]] {{gloss|type of angel, ranking above archangel}}

====Usage notes====
* Although it is a cognate to the English word "might", ''macht'' typically refers to rulership or effective power rather than physical capabilities or positive qualities. For example: in English, the phrase "a mighty king" will likely evoke the image of an effective, virtuous or strong ruler. In Dutch, however, the etymologically equivalent phrase "een machtige koning" can simply refer to a king with many resources under his control. Historically, ''macht'' referred to one's power over their body and mind. See also [[wikipedia:Will_to_power#Kraft_vs._Macht|Kraft vs. Macht]].

